From: Joe Thomas <jthomas@access.digex.net>
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 1994 23:36:17 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-11-404] ENVIRONMENT: Is the Sun "exploding"? Was: Nature's real plan

On Fri, 25 Nov 1994, Amara Graps wrote:

[Interesting stellar evolution stuff deleted]

> By careful spectroscopic comparison with other G stars, the Sun has been
> classified as a weak-line 4.6 billion-year-old star, i.e. a relatively
> old Population I star. (An age of 5 billion years is thought of as
> representative of the oldest of what astronomers call Population I
> stars.)

> Have you been following the recent reports of the Hubble Observations
> that have resulted in newer calculations of the Hubble constant?
> These newer Hubble constant calculations show the age of the Universe
> to be about 8 billion years old. Astronomers used to place the oldest
> stars (Population II) as 10-20 billion years old. How can the age of
> the Universe be younger than that of the oldest stars? The astronomers
> are in a tizzy about this, as you can imagine.

I saw this result, and wondered about its implications regarding the fate 
of intelligent life.  To summarize an old arguement, if intelligent life 
could have existed for a long time (billions of years), and yet we see no 
evidence of it in the universe except for us, one of two things is true:  
either intelligent life is an incredible rarity and we're the first 
example, or intelligent life is inherently unstable and we're doomed.

Vernor Vinge tried to take another way out in his Realtime stories, where 
the singularity leaves in its wake nothing observable by mere humans.  
That just doesn't seem reasonable to me.  Surely there'd be some 
evidence; I'd guess it would look like green goo to those not part of 
it.

But if we're just lucky enough to be the first intelligence, then there's 
a lot more cause for optimism about our eventual fate.  If life itself 
couldn't have evolved anywhere much before it did on Earth, and if we got 
just a little lucky on how fast intelligence evolved, there's not so much 
reason to be concerned about the silence in the sky.

Is this a reasonable hope?  It seems that maybe Earth was nearly first in 
supporting life, but then it took about 4 billion years for intelligence to 
arise.  That's a *long* time...

Joe

