From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 1994 15:02:03 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-394] PREDICTION: a crucial tool in the Extropian kit
XRef: Response to Message #94-11-362 (EdRegis@aol.com)

Ed Regis:
> happening in 2010, 2040, or whatever, it ought to be recognized that all 
> such forecasts are mere guesswork.  And no matter how they turn out, 
> they have little or no effect on our lives today, so what's the point, 
> really?  

Some such forecasts can be quite accurate.  For example, astronomers
can predict the trajectory of Jupiter quite accurately, and furthermore
we can say with very high confidence that technological disturbance of that
orbit by 2010 is quite improbable.   I've developed a strong method
of historical projection that is based, mostly informally at this
point,  on change in information distance over time.  The rate of 
change of information distance in technology (designs, tooling interfaces, 
etc.) is roughly proportional to the number and degree of educated
humans.  In the history of technology, large amounts of useful information 
have never been created in a short period of time.  Information must be
heavily digested, cross-referenced, and recombined in numerous failed
experiments before useful forms, which are substantially similar
to previous forms, appear.  Slow change in information will continue
to be the main objective phenomenon underlying human civilization.
Even as we achieve greater computing power, smart drugs. etc. 
the amounts of information processed per unit time will increase,
but tradeoffs, will always exist, for example the fundamental
tradeoffs in computing between memory and cycles, between security
and simplicity, between distortion and generalization, etc.  
Limits always must be pressed against.  "Singularity" is mere 
millenarian superstition.

Over the entire history of civilization, information distance has
never changed dramatically in a short period of time.  (I challenge 
readers to provide counterexamples).  Information distance projections 
are more accurate than mere guesswork, which in turn in more accurate 
than wishful thinking; no trend goes on for long without
being substiantially altered, usually limited, by other phenomena.

I can predict with reasonable confidence that the major religions in 
the world will still be Christianity, Islam, Hinduism and Bhuddism, and
that C (including the C++ variant) will still be a major computer programming 
langauge in 2010.  By analyzing R&D economics (cost/information distance) 
I can confidentally say that launch costs will not be dramatically lower 
than today, that Drexlerian nanotechnology will still be a distant (and 
perhaps wrongly discredited) dream, in 2010.  New developments in
biotechnology, software, finance, business organization, etc. are
less predictable, although I see more solid smart drugs results than life 
extension results, and what I call the "jurisdiction independent 
multinational small business", based primarily on networking software, 
looks quite quite promising economically, although quite risky.  
Politics is the least predictable of all, but my reading of the feedback 
loops says we've entered a period of stability where the trend towards 
socialism has been largely reversed, but the road back to liberty will 
be a long one.  We will have unpredictable arms races, for example
in privacy where cryptography and jurisidictional arbitrage will
go up against panoptic marketing databases and national ID cards on the 
other.

Optimism is a tenet of Extropianism, but to base hopes of immortality, 
self-transformation, etc. on wishful thinking rather than solid 
theoretical applied science and information-distance projection, is 
hardly useful, and hardly in keeping with the Extropian value
of science over faith and superstition.

Even with this new tool of information distance projection, Ed's comment 
about unpredictability is correct insofar as projecting specific dates 
in the far future for many important technologies are concerned.  However, 
knowing that things like nanotechnology, cell repair machines, cryonics 
reanimation, uploading, etc. are significantly possible over the next 
several hundred years, has _profound_ implications on the rational 
decisions we make today.  

I'd also like to reiterate, in response to Reilly Jones, that
that recognizing the consequences of these far future technologies 
is the core new element in Extropianism.  All the other elements
of Extropianism, while they may be considered important, are
borrowed.  For example, philosophy from Nietchze, Bartley, etc.
One could imagine using the core Extropian contribution and applying
other philosophies, although perhaps Nietchze and Bartley fit
better.  Personally, I don't need to buy whole hog into all these other
elements to be quite inspired by the core Extropian breakthrough,
so I don't consider them as important to Extropiniasm per se.

Also, some folks have complained that Extropianism emphasizes the
far future too much.  But in fact the original contribution of 
Extropianism has to do with the far future and its consequences
on our prospects starting in the here and now.  Extropianism without
these crucial insights about the far future would not be Extropianism,
it would be just another dead end fad popularization of philosophy.  I 
do agree that transforming our current lives, in light of those insights
about the far future, should recieve more emphasis, but we cannot
abandon exploration of the shape of that far future without 
abandoning Extropianism.

