From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Sat, 26 Nov 94 13:46:48 EST
Subject: [#94-11-406] GENETICS: Governmental power and selection

A few weeks ago James Baker said:

>I would think influencing the gender of offspring would be more popular in
>the cultures that value maleness. I have imagined that this would
>eventually lead to a great imballence in the male/female ratio in some
>countries. (I used to live in Pakistan where a mother of a newborn baby
>girl get's more condolences than congradulations.)
>
>But I've been thinking this for 20 years and I've seen absolutely no
>evidence to support my assertion.

Demographers have noted that there is a population imbalance favoring males
in some Asian countries which is apparently deliberately induced.  I
remember reading about ``millions of missing female children''. Now there is
a new wrinkle reported in the Nov 15 New York Times which carries a story
from Reuters entitled  `New Chinese Law Prohibits Sex-Screening of Fetuses'.
The new law forbids sex identification of a fetus by technical means except
when needed on medical grounds.  

``Sex-screening, using ultrasound machines, has become popular in China
because of a tradition of preferring sons to daughters. Some parents,
allowed only one child, choose abortion when they find out that the wife is
carrying a girl. That has led to a rising imbalance in the ratio of men to
women in China.''

Strangely, the title of the article, `New Chinese Law Prohibits
Sex-Screening of Fetuses', does not mention the most interesting aspect of
the new law.  The new law has additional provisions which perfectly
illustrate what a government can do when it has the power to decide issues
of procreation.  Specifically, the new law also ``bans marriage for people
diagnosed with diseases that `may totally or partially deprive the victim of
the ability to live independently, that are highly possible to recur in
generations to come and that are medically considered inappropriate for
reproduction.'''

A government spokesmen said a list of such diseases would be published
later.  Even if one believes a gender imbalance is undesirable one must also
consider what a government with the power to stop a gender imbalance may
decide to do next.          Gregory Sullivan

