From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 94 02:43:34 EST
Subject: [#94-11-410] GENE: Preserving endangered creatures

Jay Reynolds Freeman writes:
> I suspect that the technology to do an "analog backup" of the DNA 
> of species now extant does in fact exist, perhaps by nothing fancier
> than freezing selected tissue samples from living or recently 
> deceased animals.  I am not sure whether anyone is doing so.  
> Possibly not much more than an egg and sperm bank would be required.
>

The concept of freezing tissue from endangered creatures has been proposed in
the past. As Phil G. Fraering mentioned in his post the idea has been
proposed by the physicist and science fiction writer Gregory Benford.
Drexler also suggests the possibility in Engines of Creation. The Foresight
Institute is interested in facilitating this kind of preservation. Update 4
mentions a project entitled BioArchive. Interestingly, there are
organizations freezing tissue already:

Quote from Foresight Update No 4, 10/15/88.

   We are fortunate that the task of freezing species samples was begun
   even before understanding of nanotechnology showed how they could be
   restored. Germ cells of endangered species, along with other cells
   from common animals, are stored at liquid nitrogen temperatures at the
   Center for Reproduction of Endangered Species (CRES) at the San Diego
   Zoo. This effort focuses on freezing germ cells and embryos, since
   when warmed up they are often viable without the need for cell repair
   technology. The freezers containing these treasures have been labeled
   "Frozen Zoo: Twentieth Century Ark."
   
   Dr. Barbara Durrant told Update that "Right now the Frozen Zoo
   contains cells representing virtually every mammalian species on
   Earth. Mostly, these are blood and skin cells for chromosome studies
   that help us in making breeding decisions." Dr. Durrant explained that
   quite a few bird, reptile, and amphibian species were included, but no
   insects.
   
End quote

As Price mentions preserved tissue probably need not be gametes or embryos
to allow later re-creation.  Clearly, a more comprehensive program than the
one described above would be possible, e.g., coverage of more species.  I
suspect preservation of genes alone is not ideal.  To mitigate the loss of
memetic/behavioral information one could try to use extensive video/audio
footage of the behaviors and interactions of creatures which are still alive
plus field study.

What do I mean by memes here? Some songbirds learn to sing specific songs by
listening to other songbirds. Some creatures may learn hunting methods by
watching other members of their species hunt. Some primates may learn to use
tools like twigs by watching other primates, etc.  The interactionist
viewpoint says behavior is determined by a combination of interacting genes
and environment.  Preserving DNA is great compared to not preserving DNA but
also preserving behavioral data is desirable in my opinion.
               gfs

